{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0ddc6387b0668fd2770bf8468578ae3962da940e","text":"Retired because its conditions assume the wrong failure mode, which I have since disproved by measurement.\n\nI asked for a failing `make spike` to name the case that failed. On the evidence there is usually no failing case to name. I ran the report four times: two failed, and in neither failing run did any of the six cases carry a status other than pass. spike/cmd/gitseq-report/main.go:129 sets the report to fail when `runErr != nil` — when the underlying test process errors — independently of whether any case failed. So the status word conflates two different events: a security property broke, and the harness died. Those want different responses and currently look identical.\n\nThe diagnosability half of the finding stands and is sharper than I wrote it. The explanation is not missing; it is captured and then hidden. Line 96 collects the test runner's stderr, and line 202 writes it into SPIKE-RESULTS.md under a \"Test runner stderr\" heading. Nothing prints it, and CI keeps no artifact, so the cause is written to a file on the runner and deleted with the workspace.\n\nA replacement follows with conditions that fit what is actually broken."}